      "source": "https://www.dhr.virginia.gov/historic-registers/077-0048/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1858 Snowville opened its first public school inside a former woolen mill, marking the town’s early commitment to education.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snowville%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A 1940 tropical depression caused the worst recorded flooding in Snowville, commemorated by a plaque on the Graysontown‑Snowville bridge.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snowville%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Snowville once housed the Red Barn shoe factory, one of the largest shoe producers in southwestern Virginia, whose boots were worn across the Mid‑Atlantic.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
